koha (26.06.00~1) unstable; urgency=medium

  koha-common is now a metapackage. Its daemons are supervised either by
  this package (native systemd units, the default on systemd hosts) or by
  koha-sysv (the traditional /etc/init.d/koha-common script). Upgrading a
  systemd host from the monolithic koha-common installs koha-systemd,
  stops the init-script-started daemons, enables a unit for every daemon
  of every enabled instance and starts them; no manual action is needed.

  What changes for administrators on a koha-systemd host:

  * Each daemon is its own unit (koha-plack@INSTANCE.service,
    koha-zebra@INSTANCE.service, koha-worker@INSTANCE.service, ...),
    grouped by koha@INSTANCE.target and koha.target.
    "systemctl restart koha-common" still restarts everything, and the
    koha-plack/koha-zebra/koha-worker/... helpers work as before.

  * Log files are unchanged: each daemon's stdout/stderr still lands in
    /var/log/koha/INSTANCE/NAME-output.log and NAME-error.log, and the
    files Koha writes itself (plack.log, the log4perl files, z3950.log)
    are untouched. The journal holds systemd's own messages about each
    unit ("journalctl -u koha-zebra@INSTANCE" shows why one failed).

  * The units are sandboxed (PrivateTmp, ProtectSystem=full, ProtectHome,
    NoNewPrivileges). Instances whose koha-conf.xml has an empty
    <tmp_path> should set it to /var/lib/koha/INSTANCE/tmp; see
    /usr/share/doc/koha-systemd/README.Debian for details and how to
    relax a setting with a drop-in.

  * SIP is enabled per instance with "koha-sip --enable"; the
    /var/lib/koha/INSTANCE/sip.enabled flag file is no longer read on
    systemd hosts.

 -- Koha Development Team <koha-devel@lists.koha-community.org>  Tue, 15 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000
